import{at as e,n as t,rt as n,xt as r}from"./chunks/framework.DIOWbp6D.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Bibliography","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"ecosystem/bibliography.md","filePath":"ecosystem/bibliography.md"}`),a={name:`ecosystem/bibliography.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="bibliography" tabindex="-1">Bibliography <a class="header-anchor" href="#bibliography" aria-label="Permalink to “Bibliography”">​</a></h1><p>These academic works are a major inspiration for Handl. There is already a substantial body of mathematical groundwork for arrows via category theory: that is what we are drawing on.</p><p>Foundational work behind the arrow calculus and the categorical semantics the kernel is built on.</p><h2 id="arrows" tabindex="-1">Arrows <a class="header-anchor" href="#arrows" aria-label="Permalink to “Arrows”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>John Hughes</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0167-6423(99)00023-4" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Generalising Monads to Arrows</em></a> (2000, <em>Science of Computer Programming</em>). Introduced arrows and their core combinators (<code>arr</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>first</code>).</li><li><strong>Ross Paterson</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/507635.507664" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>A New Notation for Arrows</em></a> (2001, ICFP). Formalized proc notation, algebraic laws, and categorical correspondences.</li><li><strong>Sam Lindley, Philip Wadler &amp; Jeremy Yallop</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S095679680999027X" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>The Arrow Calculus</em></a> (2010, <em>Journal of Functional Programming</em>). Defined a formal typed calculus and equational semantics for arrows.</li></ul><h2 id="categorical-semantics" tabindex="-1">Categorical semantics <a class="header-anchor" href="#categorical-semantics" aria-label="Permalink to “Categorical semantics”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Eugenio Moggi</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(91)90052-4" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Notions of Computation and Monads</em></a> (1991, <em>Information and Computation</em>). Monads as notions of computation.</li><li><strong>John Power &amp; Edmund Robinson</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129597002375" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Premonoidal Categories and Notions of Computation</em></a> (1997, <em>Mathematical Structures in Computer Science</em>).</li><li><strong>John Power &amp; Hayo Thielecke</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48523-6_59" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Closed Freyd- and κ-categories</em></a> (1999, ICALP). Freyd and premonoidal categories, the canonical categorical model separating pure values from effectful computations.</li><li><strong>Paul Levy</strong>: <em>Call-by-push-value</em> (PhD thesis, Queen Mary and Westfield College, University of London, 2001). Value/computation discipline that premonoidal models are built on.</li></ul><h2 id="monads-in-prof" tabindex="-1">Monads in Prof <a class="header-anchor" href="#monads-in-prof" aria-label="Permalink to “Monads in Prof”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Chris Heunen &amp; Bart Jacobs</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.entcs.2006.04.012" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Arrows, like Monads, are Monoids</em></a> (2006, ENTCS). Arrows as monoids in categories of bifunctors.</li><li><strong>Bart Jacobs, Chris Heunen &amp; Ichiro Hasuo</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796809007308" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Categorical Semantics for Arrows</em></a> (2009, <em>Journal of Functional Programming</em>). Denotational semantics via Eilenberg–Moore and Kleisli constructions (&quot;arrows are Freyd categories&quot;).</li><li><strong>Kazuyuki Asada</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/1863597.1863607" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Arrows are Strong Monads</em></a> (2010, MSFP; <a href="https://www.riec.tohoku.ac.jp/~asada/papers/arrStrMnd.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PDF</a>). Identifies <code>first</code> with strength: arrows are strong monads (promonads) in <code>Prof</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="higher-categories" tabindex="-1">Higher categories <a class="header-anchor" href="#higher-categories" aria-label="Permalink to “Higher categories”">​</a></h2><p>Handl&#39;s IR is globular and higher-categorical: programs are 1-morphisms, laws and rewrites are 2-morphisms, and transformations extend upward. These are the foundations that make &quot;all data, all dimensions&quot; coherent.</p><ul><li><strong>Michael A. Batanin</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1006/aima.1998.1724" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Monoidal Globular Categories as a Natural Environment for the Theory of Weak n-Categories</em></a> (1998, <em>Advances in Mathematics</em>). Globular operads and weak ω-categories: the canonical setting for globular higher structure.</li><li><strong>Tom Leinster</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525896" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Higher Operads, Higher Categories</em></a> (2004, Cambridge). The standard reference for generalized operads and n-categorical structures.</li><li><strong>Eugenia Cheng &amp; Aaron Lauda</strong>: <a href="https://eugeniacheng.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/cheng-lauda-guidebook.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Higher-Dimensional Categories: an Illustrated Guide Book</em></a> (2004, IMA workshop notes). A friendly tour of the competing definitions of n-category.</li><li><strong>Jacob Lurie</strong>: <a href="https://people.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/HTT.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Higher Topos Theory</em></a> (2009). ∞-categories via quasi-categories: the modern foundation for infinite-dimensional composition and coherence.</li></ul><h2 id="graded-type-theory" tabindex="-1">Graded type theory <a class="header-anchor" href="#graded-type-theory" aria-label="Permalink to “Graded type theory”">​</a></h2><p>Handl&#39;s graded effects descend from graded monads and graded algebraic theories: the grade on an operation is what the type-level literature calls a coeffect/grade.</p><ul><li><strong>Dominic Orchard, Vilem-Benjamin Liepelt &amp; Harley Eades III</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3341714" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Quantitative Program Reasoning with Graded Modal Types</em></a> (2019, PACMPL 3(ICFP), Article 110). Graded modal types as the type-level counterpart of graded effects.</li><li><strong>Satoshi Kura</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-45231-5_21" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Graded Algebraic Theories</em></a> (2020, FoSSaCS; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.06784" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2002.06784</a>). Graded algebraic and Lawvere theories, equivalent to finitary graded monads.</li><li><strong>Jeff Egger, Rasmus Ejlers Møgelberg &amp; Alex Simpson</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/logcom/exs025" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>The Enriched Effect Calculus: Syntax and Semantics</em></a> (2014, <em>Journal of Logic and Computation</em> 24(3)). Effect calculi enriched with linear logic primitives.</li><li><strong>Benjamin Moon, Harley Eades III &amp; Dominic Orchard</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-72019-3_17" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Graded Modal Dependent Type Theory</em></a> (2021, ESOP). Graded analysis of dataflow in a dependent type theory; grades applied to both terms and types.</li></ul><h2 id="effects-and-handlers-for-arrows" tabindex="-1">Effects and handlers for arrows <a class="header-anchor" href="#effects-and-handlers-for-arrows" aria-label="Permalink to “Effects and handlers for arrows”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Takahiro Sanada</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796824000066" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Algebraic Effects and Handlers for Arrows</em></a> (2024, <em>Journal of Functional Programming</em>; also <a href="https://icfp24.sigplan.org/details/icfp-2024-jfp-first-papers/1/Algebraic-effects-and-handlers-for-arrows" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ICFP 2024</a>). Extended the arrow calculus with algebraic effects and handlers via algebras over strong promonads.</li><li><strong>Takahiro Sanada, Keisuke Hoshino, Kenshin Hirai &amp; Shin-ya Katsumata</strong>: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3828704" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Programming Backpropagation with Reverse Handlers for Arrows</em></a> (2026, PACMPL 10(ICFP), Article 306; also <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18090" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv:2602.18090</a>). Modeled neural networks and backpropagation via linear reverse effect handlers, combining strong promonads with Cartesian reverse differential categories.</li></ul>`,17)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};